%feature("docstring") casadi::Callback "

User-defined Function implemented by subclassing.

A Callback is a Function whose numerical evaluation (and optionally its
derivatives) is written by the user in Python, MATLAB or C++ by overriding
virtual methods. It can be called like any Function, embedded in MX graphs,
mapped, and used inside NLPs and integrators.

A subclass must call the base constructor and then construct(name, opts)
from its own constructor; construct() queries the virtual methods below
(get_n_in, get_n_out, get_sparsity_in/out, get_name_in/out) and calls init()
and finalize(), so all data those methods need must be set before
construct() runs. Only eval() is mandatory. Defaults: one scalar input
\"i0\" and one scalar output \"o0\".

Object lifetime: the Function side holds a raw pointer to the user object
and does not own it. The user must keep the Callback object alive (in
Python: keep a reference) for as long as any Function, expression or solver
that uses it exists; otherwise evaluation fails with \"Callback object has
been deleted\". The same holds for objects returned from
get_jacobian/get_forward/get_reverse. Callback objects cannot be copied.

Derivatives: by default a Callback is not differentiable, so jacobian(),
forward/reverse sweeps and NLP solvers that need gradients fail with
\"Derivatives cannot be calculated\". Either override
has_jacobian/get_jacobian, has_forward/get_forward or
has_reverse/get_reverse, or pass the option \"enable_fd\": True to
construct() to use finite differences (\"fd_method\": \"central\" (default),
\"forward\", \"backward\" or \"smoothing\"; \"fd_options\" such as {\"h\":
1e-6} tune the step). A user-provided Jacobian is itself differentiated with
finite differences only when it is a Callback with \"enable_fd\"; a Function
built from SX/MX gives exact second derivatives.



::

  >>> class MyCallback(Callback):
  ...   def __init__(self, name, d, opts={}):
  ...     Callback.__init__(self)
  ...     self.d = d
  ...     self.construct(name, opts)
  ...   def get_n_in(self): return 1
  ...   def get_n_out(self): return 1
  ...   def eval(self, arg):
  ...     return [sin(self.d*arg[0])]
  >>> f = MyCallback(\"f\", 0.5)
  >>> f
  Callback(f:(i0)->(o0) CallbackInternal)
  >>> round(float(f(2)), 4)
  0.8415
  >>> x = MX.sym(\"x\")
  >>> f(x)
  MX(f(x){0})
  >>> try:
  ...   jacobian(f(x), x)
  ... except Exception as e:
  ...   print(\"Derivatives cannot be calculated for f\" in str(e))
  True
  >>> f = MyCallback(\"f\", 0.5, {\"enable_fd\": True})
  >>> J = Function(\"J\", [x], [jacobian(f(x), x)])
  >>> round(float(J(2)), 4)
  0.2702
  



The Python object must outlive every use of the Function:



::

  >>> class Sq(Callback):
  ...   def __init__(self, name, opts={}):
  ...     Callback.__init__(self)
  ...     self.construct(name, opts)
  ...   def eval(self, arg):
  ...     return [arg[0]**2]
  >>> x = MX.sym(\"x\")
  >>> f = Sq(\"f\")
  >>> F = Function(\"F\", [x], [f(x)])
  >>> F(3)
  DM(9)
  >>> del f
  >>> try:
  ...   F(3)
  ... except Exception as e:
  ...   print(str(e).splitlines()[-1])
  Callback object has been deleted
  



Use in an NLP with finite differences:



::

  >>> class Sq(Callback):
  ...   def __init__(self, name, opts={}):
  ...     Callback.__init__(self)
  ...     self.construct(name, opts)
  ...   def eval(self, arg):
  ...     return [arg[0]**2]
  >>> f = Sq(\"f\", {\"enable_fd\": True})
  >>> opti = Opti()
  >>> v = opti.variable()
  >>> opti.minimize(f(v-1))
  >>> opti.solver(\"ipopt\", {\"print_time\": False}, {\"print_level\": 0, \"sb\": \"yes\"})
  >>> round(opti.solve().value(v), 3)
  1.0
  





See also: Function, external



Authors: Joris Gillis, Joel Andersson

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_o0

";

%feature("docstring")  casadi::Function::jit(const std::string &name, const
std::string &body, const std::vector< std::string > &name_in, const
std::vector< std::string > &name_out, const Dict &opts=Dict()) "

Create a just-in-time compiled function from a C language string.

``body`` is the body of a C function that is compiled with the \"compiler\"
plugin (option \"compiler\", default \"shell\" i.e. the system C compiler on
most builds, or \"clang\") and loaded as an external function. This needs a
working C compiler at run time; the source and library files are removed
afterwards unless \"jit_cleanup\" is false.

Inside the body, the nonzeros of input k are available through a pointer
named name_in[k] (type const casadi_real*) and the nonzeros of output k must
be written through a pointer named name_out[k] (type casadi_real*), in
column-major order of the sparsity pattern. casadi_real is double unless the
code generator is told otherwise. By default (option \"buffered\": true)
inputs and outputs are copied through a work vector, so the pointers are
never null; with \"buffered\": false a null input means all-zero and a null
output may be skipped. Write \"return 1;\" to signal an evaluation failure.

The result has no derivatives unless option \"enable_fd\": true (finite
differences) is passed or a C body for the Jacobian is supplied with option
\"jac\" (and \"hess\" for its Jacobian).



Parameters:
    name: Function name
    body: C code of the function body, without the enclosing braces
    name_in: Input names, also the C variable names
    name_out: Output names, also the C variable names
    sparsity_in: Sparsity of each input; all scalar when omitted
    sparsity_out: Sparsity of each output; all scalar when omitted
    opts: Options; \"compiler\", \"jit_options\", \"buffered\", \"jac\", \"hess\" and the common Function options

Example (not run here because it invokes a compiler): f =
Function.jit(\"f\", \"r[0] = x[0]*x[0] + y[0];\", [\"x\", \"y\"], [\"r\"])
gives a function f:(x,y)->(r) that evaluates like any other Function.



See also: external

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_1v3

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/function.hpp#L391
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/function.cpp#L289-L296
";

%feature("docstring")  casadi::Callback::has_jacobian() const "

Return Jacobian of all input elements with respect to all output elements.

Override has_jacobian() to return true and get_jacobian() to return a
Function with inputs [all nominal inputs, all nominal outputs] and one
output per (output, input) pair, each the dense or sparse Jacobian block of
that output with respect to that input (row-major over the pairs). Use the
``inames``, ``onames`` and ``opts`` passed in: they carry the expected names
and the \"derivative_of\" option. The returned Function may be an SX/MX
Function (then higher derivatives are exact) or another Callback (keep a
reference to it).



::

  >>> class F43(Callback):
  ...   def __init__(self, name, opts={}):
  ...     Callback.__init__(self)
  ...     self.construct(name, opts)
  ...   def get_sparsity_in(self, i): return Sparsity.dense(4, 1)
  ...   def get_sparsity_out(self, i): return Sparsity.dense(3, 1)
  ...   def eval(self, arg):
  ...     a, b, c, d = vertsplit(arg[0])
  ...     return [vertcat(sin(c)*d+d**2, 2*a+c, b**2+5*c)]
  ...   def has_jacobian(self): return True
  ...   def get_jacobian(self, name, inames, onames, opts):
  ...     print(name, inames, onames)
  ...     x = SX.sym(\"x\", 4)
  ...     y = SX.sym(\"y\", Sparsity(3, 1))
  ...     a, b, c, d = vertsplit(x)
  ...     J = blockcat([[0, 0, d*cos(c), sin(c)+2*d], [2, 0, 1, 0], [0, 2*b, 5, 0]])
  ...     return Function(name, [x, y], [J], inames, onames, opts)
  >>> f = F43(\"f\")
  >>> x = MX.sym(\"x\", 4)
  >>> J = Function(\"J\", [x], [jacobian(f(x), x)])
  jac_f ['i0', 'out_o0'] ['jac_o0_i0']
  >>> J([1, 2, 0, 3])
  DM(
  [[0, 0, 3, 6],
   [2, 0, 1, 0],
   [0, 4, 5, 0]])
  



Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_oh

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/callback.hpp#L725
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/callback.cpp#L100-L102
";

