%feature("docstring")  casadi::Callback::has_reverse(casadi_int nadj) const
"

Return function that calculates adjoint derivatives.

Override has_reverse(nadj) to return true for the numbers of directions you
support and get_reverse() to return a Function with inputs [nominal inputs,
nominal outputs, one adjoint seed per output] and one adjoint sensitivity
per input. For nadj>1 the seeds and sensitivities are horizontally stacked.
Reverse mode is what gradient-based NLP solvers use for the objective
gradient. reverse(nadj) caches the result.



::

  >>> class Sq(Callback):
  ...   def __init__(self, name, opts={}):
  ...     Callback.__init__(self)
  ...     self.construct(name, opts)
  ...   def eval(self, arg):
  ...     return [arg[0]**2]
  ...   def has_reverse(self, nadj): return nadj == 1
  ...   def get_reverse(self, nadj, name, inames, onames, opts):
  ...     print(nadj, name, inames, onames)
  ...     x, y, yb = SX.sym(\"x\"), SX.sym(\"y\", Sparsity(1, 1)), SX.sym(\"yb\")
  ...     return Function(name, [x, y, yb], [2*x*yb], inames, onames, opts)
  >>> f = Sq(\"f\")
  >>> x = MX.sym(\"x\")
  >>> G = Function(\"G\", [x], [gradient(f(x), x)])
  1 adj1_f ['i0', 'out_o0', 'adj_o0'] ['adj_i0']
  >>> G(3)
  DM(6)
  



