%feature("docstring")  casadi::Function::jacobian() const "

Get a function computing all Jacobian blocks at once.

The result is named jac_<name> and takes all inputs followed by all outputs
(named out_<output>); the output values are only read when uses_output() is
true and may be passed as 0 otherwise. It returns one block per (output,
input) pair, ordered by output first: for f:(x,y)->(r,s) the outputs are
jac_r_x, jac_r_y, jac_s_x, jac_s_y. Blocks involving a non-differentiable
input or output are all-zero sparse. Each block is a size_out(o)-times-
size_in(i) matrix where the output and input are vectorized column-major;
blocks that are structurally zero have an all-zero sparsity pattern.

The result is cached: calling jacobian again returns the same object as long
as it is alive. The derivative itself is built by symbolic AD for SX and MX
functions, by the solver's own sensitivity method for solver objects, or
raises \"Derivatives cannot be calculated\" for classes without any
derivative support. For a single block, factory(\"...\", ..., [\"jac:r:x\"])
avoids computing the others.



::

  >>> x = SX.sym(\"x\"); y = SX.sym(\"y\", 2)
  >>> f = Function(\"f\", [x, y], [x*y, sin(x)], [\"x\", \"y\"], [\"r\", \"s\"])
  >>> J = f.jacobian()
  >>> print(J)
  jac_f:(x,y[2],out_r[2x1,0nz],out_s[1x1,0nz])->(jac_r_x[2],jac_r_y[2x2,2nz],...) SXFunction
  >>> J(1, [2, 3], 0, 0)
  (DM([2, 3]), DM(
  [[1, 00],
   [00, 1]]), DM(0.540302), DM([[00, 00]]))
  >>> f.jacobian().name_out()
  ['jac_r_x', 'jac_r_y', 'jac_s_x', 'jac_s_y']
  





See also: factory, forward, reverse, jac_sparsity, uses_output

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_1w6

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/function.hpp#L1601
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/function.cpp#L1068-L1074
";

