/* Import and export */ %feature("docstring") casadi::DaeBuilder "

A symbolic representation of a differential-algebraic equations model.

A DaeBuilder holds a list of uniquely named model variables with FMI-style
meta data (causality, variability, type, start value, bounds, unit, ...)
together with the model equations. The model is either built symbolically
with ``add``, ``eq``, ``when`` and friends, or imported from an FMU /
``modelDescription.xml`` (constructor with a path,
``load_fmi_description``). Numerical evaluation goes through the function
factory ``create``, which returns differentiable Function objects.

Every variable belongs to at most one CasADi category, derived from its
causality, variability and defining equation:

Variables:
==========





::

  t:      independent variable (usually time)
  c:      constants
  p:      parameters
  d:      dependent parameters (time independent)
  u:      controls
  w:      dependent variables  (time dependent)
  x:      differential states
  z:      algebraic variables
  q:      quadrature states
  y:      outputs
  



Equations:
==========





::

  differential equations: \\\\dot{x} ==  ode(...)
  algebraic equations:          0 ==  alg(...)
  quadrature equations:   \\\\dot{q} == quad(...)
  dependent parameters:         d == ddef(d_prev,p)
  dependent variables:          w == wdef(w_prev,x,z,u,p,t)
  output equations:             y == ydef(...)
  initial equations:     init_lhs == init_rhs(...)
  events:      when when_cond < 0: when_lhs := when_rhs
  



The category names (``\"x\"``, ``\"ode\"``, ...) are the strings accepted by
``all``, ``inputs``, ``outputs``, ``create`` and the other category-based
members. Internal helper variables (named ``der(x)``, ``__alg__0``,
``__assign__..``, ``__when__0``, ...) are created as equations are added;
they show up in ``all()``.



::

  >>> dae = DaeBuilder(\"rocket\")
  >>> a = dae.add(\"a\", \"parameter\", \"tunable\")
  >>> u = dae.add(\"u\", \"input\")
  >>> h = dae.add(\"h\", dict(start=0))
  >>> v = dae.add(\"v\", dict(start=0, unit=\"m/s\"))
  >>> m = dae.add(\"m\", dict(start=1))
  >>> dae.eq(dae.der(h), v)
  >>> dae.eq(dae.der(v), (u - a*v**2)/m - 9.81)
  >>> dae.eq(dae.der(m), -u**2)
  >>> dae.x(), dae.u(), dae.p()
  (['h', 'v', 'm'], ['u'], ['a'])
  >>> f = dae.create(\"f\", [\"x\", \"u\", \"p\"], [\"ode\"])
  >>> f
  Function(f:(x[3],u,p)->(ode[3]) MXFunction)
  >>> f([0, 0, 1], 1, 0.5)
  DM([0, -8.81, -1])
  





Authors: Joel Andersson

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_5c

";

%feature("docstring")  casadi::DaeBuilder::time() const "

Expression for independent variable (usually time)

Only available when an independent variable has been declared explicitly
with ``add(name, \"independent\")`` or imported from the model description;
otherwise an error is raised. Without an explicit declaration a hidden
variable named ``\"time\"`` exists in ``all()`` but is not categorized as
``t``.



::

  >>> dae = DaeBuilder(\"m\")
  >>> t = dae.add(\"t\", \"independent\")
  >>> x = dae.add(\"x\")
  >>> dae.time(), dae.has_t(), dae.t_new()
  (MX(t), True, ['t'])
  >>> dae = DaeBuilder(\"m\")
  >>> x = dae.add(\"x\")
  >>> dae.all(), dae.has_t(), dae.t_new()
  (['time', 'x'], False, [])
  >>> try:
  ...   dae.time()
  ... except Exception as e:
  ...   print(\"No explicit time variable\" in str(e))
  True
  





See also: has_t, t_new

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_2by

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/dae_builder.hpp#L264
