%feature("docstring")  casadi::Function::jit(const std::string &name, const
std::string &body, const std::vector< std::string > &name_in, const
std::vector< std::string > &name_out, const Dict &opts=Dict()) "

Create a just-in-time compiled function from a C language string.

``body`` is the body of a C function that is compiled with the \"compiler\"
plugin (option \"compiler\", default \"shell\" i.e. the system C compiler on
most builds, or \"clang\") and loaded as an external function. This needs a
working C compiler at run time; the source and library files are removed
afterwards unless \"jit_cleanup\" is false.

Inside the body, the nonzeros of input k are available through a pointer
named name_in[k] (type const casadi_real*) and the nonzeros of output k must
be written through a pointer named name_out[k] (type casadi_real*), in
column-major order of the sparsity pattern. casadi_real is double unless the
code generator is told otherwise. By default (option \"buffered\": true)
inputs and outputs are copied through a work vector, so the pointers are
never null; with \"buffered\": false a null input means all-zero and a null
output may be skipped. Write \"return 1;\" to signal an evaluation failure.

The result has no derivatives unless option \"enable_fd\": true (finite
differences) is passed or a C body for the Jacobian is supplied with option
\"jac\" (and \"hess\" for its Jacobian).



Parameters:
    name: Function name
    body: C code of the function body, without the enclosing braces
    name_in: Input names, also the C variable names
    name_out: Output names, also the C variable names
    sparsity_in: Sparsity of each input; all scalar when omitted
    sparsity_out: Sparsity of each output; all scalar when omitted
    opts: Options; \"compiler\", \"jit_options\", \"buffered\", \"jac\", \"hess\" and the common Function options

Example (not run here because it invokes a compiler): f =
Function.jit(\"f\", \"r[0] = x[0]*x[0] + y[0];\", [\"x\", \"y\"], [\"r\"])
gives a function f:(x,y)->(r) that evaluates like any other Function.



See also: external

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_1v3

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/function.hpp#L391
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/function.cpp#L289-L296
";

%feature("docstring") casadi::Function::Function(const std::string &name,
const std::vector< SX > &ex_in, const std::vector< SX > &ex_out, const Dict
&opts=Dict()) "

Construct a function from SX input and output expressions.

The result is an SXFunction: a flat list of scalar instructions that is
cheap to evaluate but has no notion of matrix structure (see expand for the
difference with an MXFunction).

Every symbolic primitive that appears in ``ex_out`` must be present in
``ex_in``, otherwise construction fails with \"Initialization failed since
variables [...] are free\" (the symbol was forgotten in the input list).
Pass option \"allow_free\": true to construct anyway; such a function can
only be called symbolically. Each input must be a purely symbolic matrix
(symbols or a concatenation of symbols); an output may be any expression,
including constants and empty matrices. The sparsity patterns of the
expressions become sparsity_in and sparsity_out.



Parameters:
    name: Display name, used in error messages and generated code. Must start with a letter and contain only letters, digits and non-consecutive underscores (see check_name); otherwise an error is raised.
    ex_in: List of input expressions, each a symbolic SX
    ex_out: List of output expressions
    name_in: Names of the inputs; defaults to i0, i1,...; duplicates raise an error unless option \"allow_duplicate_io_names\" is set
    name_out: Names of the outputs; defaults to o0, o1,...
    opts: Options, see the table below. The common ones are \"cse\" (eliminate common subexpressions), \"live_variables\", \"is_diff_in\"/\"is_diff_out\", \"default_in\", \"allow_free\", \"jit\"

The dict overload takes a single map from name to expression; ``name_in``
and ``name_out`` select which keys are inputs and outputs, in that order. A
key that is in neither list raises \"Unknown dictionary entry\". A missing
key gives an empty (0-by-0) input or output.



::

  >>> x = SX.sym(\"x\"); y = SX.sym(\"y\", 2)
  >>> f = Function(\"f\", [x, y], [x*y, sin(x)])
  >>> f
  Function(f:(i0,i1[2])->(o0[2],o1) SXFunction)
  >>> f = Function(\"f\", [x, y], [x*y, sin(x)], [\"x\", \"y\"], [\"r\", \"s\"])
  >>> f.name_in(), f.name_out()
  (['x', 'y'], ['r', 's'])
  >>> g = Function(\"g\", {\"x\": x, \"y\": y, \"r\": x*y}, [\"x\", \"y\"], [\"r\"])
  >>> g
  Function(g:(x,y[2])->(r[2]) SXFunction)
  >>> p = SX.sym(\"p\")
  >>> try:
  ...   Function(\"h\", [x], [x*p])
  ... except RuntimeError as e:
  ...   print(\"Initialization failed\" in str(e), \"[p] are free\" in str(e))
  True True
  >>> h = Function(\"h\", [x], [x*p], {\"allow_free\": True})
  >>> h.get_free()
  ['p']
  >>> try:
  ...   Function(\"my func\", [x], [x])
  ... except RuntimeError as e:
  ...   print(\"Function name is not valid\" in str(e))
  True
  





See also: expand, check_name, has_free

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_1v0

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/function.hpp#L234
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/function.cpp#L96-L100
";

%feature("docstring") casadi::Function::Function(const std::string &name,
const std::vector< MX > &ex_in, const std::vector< MX > &ex_out, const Dict
&opts=Dict()) "

Construct a function from MX input and output expressions.

The result is an MXFunction: a graph of matrix-valued nodes that may embed
calls to other Function objects (solvers, integrators, SX functions, ...).
Compared with an SXFunction it uses less memory and supports every
operation, but is slower per node to evaluate; call expand to convert when
all nodes are expandable.

The rules on free variables, naming and the dict overload are the same as
for the SX constructor. Additional MX-only options: \"post_expand\" (expand
right after construction) and \"post_expand_options\".



Parameters:
    name: Display name, must satisfy check_name
    ex_in: List of symbolic MX inputs
    ex_out: List of MX output expressions
    name_in: Input names; defaults to i0, i1,...
    name_out: Output names; defaults to o0, o1,...
    opts: Options, see the table below



::

  >>> x = MX.sym(\"x\", 2); A = MX.sym(\"A\", 2, 2)
  >>> f = Function(\"f\", [A, x], [A @ x, norm_2(x)], [\"A\", \"x\"], [\"Ax\", \"nrm\"])
  >>> f
  Function(f:(A[2x2],x[2])->(Ax[2],nrm) MXFunction)
  >>> f.class_name(), f.n_instructions()
  ('MXFunction', 7)
  >>> f.expand()
  Function(f:(A[2x2],x[2])->(Ax[2],nrm) SXFunction)
  >>> g = Function(\"g\", [x], [x], {\"post_expand\": True})
  >>> g.class_name()
  'SXFunction'
  





See also: expand, has_free

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_1v1

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/function.hpp#L299
