%feature("docstring")  casadi::Function::expand() const "

Convert an MXFunction into an equivalent SXFunction.

Evaluates the function symbolically with SX inputs and builds an SXFunction
from the result: every matrix node is unrolled into scalar instructions.
Evaluation of the result is typically faster per operation, at the cost of
memory proportional to the number of scalar operations. The name,
input/output names and the options of the original function (other than the
SX-specific ones given in ``opts``) are kept.

Fails when the graph contains a node that has no scalar expansion, with
\"eval_sx not defined for ...\": for example calls to external functions or
linear solves (solve, inv) with a numerical plugin; use the \"symbolicqr\"
plugin for those. Fails when the function has free variables. An SXFunction
is returned unchanged (as a copy).



Parameters:
    name: Name of the new function; defaults to the current name
    opts: Options for the SXFunction constructor, e.g. \"live_variables\", \"cse\"



Returns:
    SXFunction



::

  >>> x = MX.sym(\"x\", 2)
  >>> f = Function(\"f\", [x], [x.T @ x, 2*x], [\"x\"], [\"q\", \"y\"])
  >>> f, f.n_instructions()
  (Function(f:(x[2])->(q,y[2]) MXFunction), 7)
  >>> g = f.expand()
  >>> g, g.n_instructions()
  (Function(f:(x[2])->(q,y[2]) SXFunction), 10)
  >>> f([1, 2]), g([1, 2])
  ((DM(5), DM([2, 4])), (DM(5), DM([2, 4])))
  >>> f.expand(\"f_sx\", {\"live_variables\": False})
  Function(f_sx:(x[2])->(q,y[2]) SXFunction)
  >>> A = MX.sym(\"A\", 2, 2)
  >>> h = Function(\"h\", [A, x], [solve(A, x)])
  >>> try:
  ...   h.expand()
  ... except RuntimeError as e:
  ...   print(\"eval_sx not defined\" in str(e))
  True
  >>> Function(\"h\", [A, x], [solve(A, x, \"symbolicqr\")]).expand().class_name()
  'SXFunction'
  





See also: Function::Function, transform

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_1v5

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/function.hpp#L475
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/function.cpp#L312-L315
";

%feature("docstring")  casadi::Function::transform(const Dict &opts=Dict())
const "

Rebuild the function after graph simplification or other passes.

Only defined for SXFunction and MXFunction. The result has the same
signature, names and options; only the expression graph differs.

The dict form runs a single \"simplify\" pass whose tasks are selected with
boolean options, all defaulting to on except combine_terms:

- empty_inputs: an input whose symbols do not occur in any output is replaced by an all-zero sparse matrix of the same shape (its nnz becomes 0)
- cse: common subexpression elimination
- ref_count: drop unreferenced nodes, repeated until a fixed point
- const_folding: evaluate constant subexpressions
- combine_terms: merge like terms of sums



Unknown keys raise an error.

The passes form runs an explicit list of passes in order. Each pass is a
list whose first entry is the verb:

- [\"simplify\", task,...] with tasks from the list above; an integer before a task is its run count (N>0: N times, 0: until the graph stops shrinking)
- [\"expand\"]: expand to an SXFunction
- [\"external\", library, operation] or [..., opts]: a transform from a plugin library



Here ``opts`` accepts only \"verbose\"; passing the boolean task options
raises an error. With \"verbose\": true the node and instruction counts
after each pass are printed.



Parameters:
    fname: Name of the result; defaults to the current name
    passes: Explicit list of passes
    opts: Dict form: task booleans and \"verbose\"; passes form: \"verbose\" only



::

  >>> z = MX.sym(\"z\"); w = MX.sym(\"w\")
  >>> f = Function(\"f\", [z, w], [sin(z)*cos(z) + sin(z)*cos(z)], [\"z\", \"w\"], [\"r\"])
  >>> f, f.n_instructions()
  (Function(f:(z,w)->(r) MXFunction), 9)
  >>> g = f.transform()
  >>> g, g.n_instructions()
  (Function(f:(z,w[1x1,0nz])->(r) MXFunction), 6)
  >>> f.transform([[\"simplify\", \"cse\"]]).n_instructions()
  6
  >>> f.transform(\"fe\", [[\"expand\"]])
  Function(fe:(z,w)->(r) SXFunction)
  >>> try:
  ...   f.transform([[\"nope\"]])
  ... except RuntimeError as e:
  ...   print(\"unknown pass verb\" in str(e))
  True
  





See also: expand

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_2ig

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/function.hpp#L552
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/function.cpp#L359-L361
";

