%feature("docstring")  casadi::Function::call(const std::vector< DM > &arg,
std::vector< DM > &res, bool always_inline=false, bool never_inline=false)
const "

Evaluate with a list or a dict of arguments; the result has the same form.

This is the general evaluation entry point; the call operator (f(...) in
Python, C++ and MATLAB) is a shorthand for it. Argument lists must have
exactly n_in() entries. A dict maps input names to values; missing inputs
take default_in, unknown names raise an error. The result is a list of
n_out() values, or a dict keyed by output name.

The argument type selects the evaluation mode:

- DM (or numbers, lists, numpy arrays): numerical evaluation, returns DM
- SX: symbolic evaluation producing an SX expression; an MX function is expanded on the fly (fails if it contains non-expandable nodes)
- MX: symbolic evaluation producing a call node f(args){k} in the MX graph, unless the function is inlined



Shapes: each argument must have the input shape, or be a scalar (broadcast
to all entries), a transposed vector, an empty matrix (treated as all
zeros), or N-by-(P*M) for an N-by-M input (P evaluations, outputs stacked
horizontally; other arguments are repeated). Anything else raises an error
that lists these rules. Sparsity is projected: entries outside sparsity_in
are ignored. When all inputs are scalar and the arguments are matrices of
one common shape, the function is applied elementwise. Numerical evaluation
of a function with free variables raises \"Cannot evaluate ... since
variables [...] are free\".



Parameters:
    arg: List or dict of arguments, all of the same type
    res: Output: list or dict of results
    always_inline: MX only: expand the call into the caller's graph instead of creating a call node (SX functions are then inlined as scalar operations); combined with never_inline raises an error
    never_inline: SX only: keep the call as an opaque call node inside the SX expression instead of inlining it



::

  >>> x = SX.sym(\"x\"); y = SX.sym(\"y\", 2)
  >>> f = Function(\"f\", [x, y], [x*y, sin(x)], [\"x\", \"y\"], [\"r\", \"s\"])
  >>> f.call([2, [3, 4]])
  [DM([6, 8]), DM(0.909297)]
  >>> f.call({\"x\": 2})
  {'r': DM([0, 0]), 's': DM(0.909297)}
  >>> f(2, [3, 4])
  (DM([6, 8]), DM(0.909297))
  >>> f(x=2, y=[3, 4])
  {'r': DM([6, 8]), 's': DM(0.909297)}
  >>> f(2, DM([[3, 5], [4, 6]]))
  (DM(
  [[6, 10],
   [8, 12]]), DM([[0.909297, 0.909297]]))
  >>> f(DM(0, 0), 3)
  (DM([0, 0]), DM(0))
  >>> z = MX.sym(\"z\"); w = MX.sym(\"w\", 2)
  >>> f.call([z, w])
  [MX(f(z, w){0}), MX(f(z, w){1})]
  >>> f.call([z, w], True)
  [MX(vertcat((z*w[0]), (z*w[1]))), MX(sin(z))]
  >>> a = SX.sym(\"a\")
  >>> f.call([a, [1, 2]])
  [SX([a, (2.*a)]), SX(sin(a))]
  >>> try:
  ...   f(1)
  ... except RuntimeError as e:
  ...   print(\"Incorrect number of inputs: Expected 2, got 1\" in str(e))
  True
  >>> try:
  ...   f(1, [1, 2, 3])
  ... except RuntimeError as e:
  ...   print(\"Input 1 (y) has mismatching shape. Got 3-by-1\" in str(e))
  True
  





See also: default_in, convert_in, map, mapsum

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_1w7

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/function.hpp#L1714
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/function.cpp#L509-L516
";

