";

%feature("docstring")  casadi::Function::map(casadi_int n, const std::string
&parallelization="serial") const "

Create a function that evaluates this function n times on stacked arguments.

For f:(a, p)->(s) the result is F:(A, P)->(S) with A = horzcat(a0, ...,
a_(n-1)), the same for P, and S = horzcat(f(a0, p0), ..., f(a_(n-1),
p_(n-1))). Every input and output is widened to n times its number of
columns; when calling, a non-widened argument is repeated for all
evaluations. The graph size and construction time are independent of n. For
n == 1 the function itself is returned; n < 1 raises \"Degenerate map
operation\".



Parameters:
    n: Number of evaluations
    parallelization: How the evaluations are run: \"serial\" (a Map node evaluated in a loop; cached, so repeated calls return the same object), \"openmp\" (OpenMP parallel loop, requires an OpenMP build), \"thread\" (std::thread per evaluation), \"unroll\" (n explicit calls in an MXFunction), \"inline\" (like unroll with the calls inlined). Other values raise an error.
    max_num_threads: With \"thread\" or \"openmp\": split the n evaluations into at most this many parallel chunks, each a serial map



::

  >>> x = SX.sym(\"x\"); p = SX.sym(\"p\")
  >>> f = Function(\"f\", [x, p], [p*x**2], [\"x\", \"p\"], [\"y\"])
  >>> F = f.map(3)
  >>> F
  Function(map3_f:(x[1x3],p[1x3])->(y[1x3]) Map)
  >>> F(DM([[1, 2, 3]]), 2)
  DM([[2, 8, 18]])
  >>> f.map(4, \"thread\", 2)
  Function(threadmap2_map2_f:(x[1x4],p[1x4])->(y[1x4]) ThreadMap)
  >>> f.map(3, \"unroll\")
  Function(f_3:(x[1x3],p[1x3])->(y[1x3]) MXFunction)
  >>> f.map(1) is f, f.map(1).name()
  (False, 'f')
  





See also: mapaccum, mapsum, call

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_1wj

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/function.hpp#L2026
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/function.cpp#L861-L896
";

%feature("docstring")  casadi::Function::generate_in(const std::string
&fname, const std::vector< DM > &arg) "

Write or read a text file with the nonzeros of all inputs.

With arguments, writes the nonzeros of ``arg`` (one value per line, in input
order) to ``fname``; this is the input format read by code generated with
option \"main\". Without arguments, reads such a file back into a list of DM
with the input sparsities. The arguments follow the shape rules of call
(scalars are broadcast); a file with the wrong number of values raises a
dimension error.



::

  >>> import os
  >>> f = Function(\"f\", [SX.sym(\"x\"), SX.sym(\"y\", 2)], [SX(0)])
  >>> f.generate_in(\"f_in.txt\", [1, [2, 3]])
  >>> open(\"f_in.txt\").read().split()
  ['1.0000000000000000e+00', '2.0000000000000000e+00', '3.0000000000000000e+00']
  >>> f.generate_in(\"f_in.txt\")
  [DM(1), DM([2, 3])]
  >>> os.remove(\"f_in.txt\")
  





