%feature("docstring")  casadi::Function::mapsum(const std::vector< MX > &x,
const std::string &parallelization="serial") const "

Evaluate on horizontally stacked MX arguments and sum the results.

Each argument is either the input itself or n copies stacked horizontally
(N-by-(n*M) for an N-by-M input); the largest such n sets the number of
evaluations and non-stacked arguments are shared across evaluations. Returns
the sum over the n evaluations of each output, as MX expressions calling an
internal map-and-reduce function.



Parameters:
    x: List of n_in()  MX arguments
    parallelization: \"serial\" only; other values raise an error



::

  >>> x = SX.sym(\"x\"); y = SX.sym(\"y\")
  >>> f = Function(\"f\", [x, y], [x*y, x - y], [\"x\", \"y\"], [\"p\", \"d\"])
  >>> p, d = f.mapsum([MX.sym(\"a\"), MX.sym(\"b\", 1, 3)])
  >>> p
  MX(mapsum(a, b){0})
  >>> f.mapsum([2, DM([[1, 2, 3]])])
  [MX(mapsum(2, [[1, 2, 3]]){0}), MX(mapsum(2, [[1, 2, 3]]){1})]
  >>> evalf(f.mapsum([2, DM([[1, 2, 3]])])[0])
  DM(12)
  





See also: map, call

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_1wh

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/function.hpp#L1881
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/function.cpp#L908-L915
";

%feature("docstring")  casadi::Function::slice(const std::string &name,
const std::vector< casadi_int > &order_in, const std::vector< casadi_int >
&order_out, const Dict &opts=Dict()) const "

Create a function with a subset or reordering of the inputs and outputs.

The expressions are unchanged; only the lists of inputs and outputs are
rearranged. Indices may be repeated or omitted. An omitted input that an
output depends on makes the result have free variables (construction then
fails unless \"allow_free\" is in ``opts``). Classes other than SXFunction
and MXFunction are wrapped first.



Parameters:
    name: Name of the result
    order_in: Indices of the original inputs to keep, in the new order
    order_out: Indices of the original outputs to keep, in the new order
    opts: Options for the new function



::

  >>> x = SX.sym(\"x\"); y = SX.sym(\"y\")
  >>> f = Function(\"f\", [x, y], [x + y, x - y, 2*x], [\"x\", \"y\"], [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"])
  >>> g = f.slice(\"g\", [1, 0], [2, 0])
  >>> g, g(1, 2)
  (Function(g:(y,x)->(c,a) SXFunction), (DM(4), DM(3)))
  >>> f.slice(\"h\", [0], [2])
  Function(h:(x)->(c) SXFunction)
  





See also: factory

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_1wl

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/function.hpp#L2122
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/function.cpp#L899-L906
";

%feature("docstring")  casadi::Function::forward(casadi_int nfwd) const "

Get a function computing ``nfwd`` forward directional derivatives.

The result is named fwd<nfwd>_<name> and has n_in + n_out + n_in inputs: the
original inputs, the original outputs (out_<name>, read only when
uses_output() is true) and the forward seeds fwd_<input>, each with nfwd
times the columns of the input. Its n_out outputs fwd_<output> are the
forward sensitivities, nfwd stacked horizontally. Uses the class' own
forward mode when available (\"enable_forward\"), finite differences when
\"enable_fd\" is set, or otherwise the full Jacobian; raises \"Derivatives
cannot be calculated\" when none applies. Results are cached while alive.
``nfwd`` must be positive.



::

  >>> x = SX.sym(\"x\"); y = SX.sym(\"y\", 2)
  >>> f = Function(\"f\", [x, y], [x*y, sin(x)], [\"x\", \"y\"], [\"r\", \"s\"])
  >>> F = f.forward(1)
  >>> print(F)
  fwd1_f:(x,y[2],out_r[2x1,0nz],out_s[1x1,0nz],fwd_x,fwd_y[2])->(...) SXFunction
  >>> F(1, [2, 3], 0, 0, 1, [0, 0])
  (DM([2, 3]), DM(0.540302))
  >>> print(f.forward(2))
  fwd2_f:(x,y[2],out_r[2x1,0nz],out_s[1x1,0nz],fwd_x[1x2],fwd_y[2x2])->(...) SXFunction
  





See also: reverse, jacobian, factory

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_1wq

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/function.hpp#L2327
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/function.cpp#L1324-L1330
";

%feature("docstring")  casadi::Function::reverse(casadi_int nadj) const "

Get a function computing ``nadj`` reverse (adjoint) directional derivatives.

The result is named adj<nadj>_<name> and has n_in + n_out + n_out inputs:
the original inputs, the original outputs (out_<name>) and the adjoint seeds
adj_<output>, each with nadj times the columns of the output. Its n_in
outputs adj_<input> are the adjoint sensitivities, nadj stacked
horizontally. Falls back to the full Jacobian when the class has no reverse
mode; raises when it has no derivatives at all. Results are cached while
alive. ``nadj`` must be positive.



::

  >>> x = SX.sym(\"x\"); y = SX.sym(\"y\", 2)
  >>> f = Function(\"f\", [x, y], [x*y, sin(x)], [\"x\", \"y\"], [\"r\", \"s\"])
  >>> R = f.reverse(1)
  >>> R
  Function(adj1_f:(x,y[2],out_r[2x1,0nz],out_s[1x1,0nz],adj_r[2],adj_s)->(adj_x,adj_y[2])
  SXFunction)
  >>> R(1, [2, 3], 0, 0, [1, 1], 0)
  (DM(5), DM([1, 1]))
  





See also: forward, jacobian, factory

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_1wr

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/function.hpp#L2362
