%feature("docstring")  casadi::GenericMatrixCommon::jtimes(const MatType
&ex, const MatType &arg, const MatType &v, bool tr=false, const Dict
&opts=Dict()) "

Calculate the Jacobian and multiply by a vector from the right.

This is equivalent to ``mul(jacobian(ex, arg), v)`` or ``mul(jacobian(ex,
arg).T, v)`` for tr set to false and true respectively. If contrast to these
expressions, it will use directional derivatives which is typically (but not
necessarily) more efficient if the complete Jacobian is not needed and v has
few rows.

The matrix shapes of ``ex`` and ``arg`` are kept: ``v`` must have the shape
of ``arg`` (or of ``ex`` when ``tr`` is true), and several directions can be
passed at once by concatenating them horizontally, i.e. v.size2() a multiple
of arg.size2(). The result is ex.size1()-by-ex.size2()*n_dir
(arg.size1()-by-arg.size2()*n_dir when ``tr`` is true). An inconsistent
shape of ``v`` raises an error.



::

  >>> x = SX.sym(\"x\"); y = SX.sym(\"y\")
  >>> ex = vertcat(x*y, x**2); arg = vertcat(x, y)
  >>> jtimes(ex, arg, DM([1, 0]))
  SX([y, (2.*x)])
  >>> jtimes(ex, arg, DM([1, 0]), True)
  SX([y, x])
  >>> jtimes(ex, arg, DM.eye(2))
  SX(
  [[y, x],
   [(2.*x), 0]])
  >>> jtimes(ex, arg, SX.sym(\"v\", 2))
  SX([((y*v_0)+(x*v_1)), ((2.*x)*v_0)])
  





See also: jacobian, forward, reverse

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_1cw

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/generic_matrix.hpp#L2586
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/generic_matrix.hpp#L2586-L2589
";

