/* Construct symbolic primitives */

/* The \"sym\" function is intended to work in a similar way as \"sym\" used
in the Symbolic Toolbox for Matlab but instead creating a CasADi symbolic
primitive.

*/ %feature("docstring") casadi::MatrixCommon "

Sparse matrix class. SX and DM are specializations.

General sparse matrix class that is designed with the idea that \"everything
is a matrix\", that is, also scalars and vectors. This philosophy makes it
easy to use and to interface in particularly with Python and Matlab/Octave.
Index starts with 0. Index vec happens as follows: (rr, cc) -> k =
rr+cc*size1() Vectors are column vectors. The storage format is Compressed
Column Storage (CCS), similar to that used for sparse matrices in Matlab,
but unlike this format, we do allow for elements to be structurally non-zero
but numerically zero. Matrix<Scalar> is polymorphic with a
std::vector<Scalar> that contain all non-identical-zero elements. The
sparsity can be accessed with Sparsity& sparsity() SX holds symbolic scalar
entries (SXElem), DM holds doubles. A structural zero prints as 00, a
numerical zero as 0. The structural nonzeros are available as a flat column-
major vector with nonzeros().



::

  >>> A = DM([[1, 2], [3, 4]])
  >>> A.sparsity().dim(), A.nonzeros()
  ('2x2', [1.0, 3.0, 2.0, 4.0])
  >>> S = DM(Sparsity.diag(2))
  >>> S
  DM(
  [[1, 00],
   [00, 1]])
  >>> S.nnz(), S.numel()
  (2, 4)
  





Authors: Joel Andersson

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_18e

";

%feature("docstring")  casadi::Matrix< double >::get(Matrix< double > &m,
bool ind1, const Slice &rr) const "

Get elements by a single (linear) index.

Backs the Python expressions x[k] and x[sp]. The index addresses elements,
not nonzeros, in column-major order: element (i, j) has linear index i +
j*size1(). Negative indices count from the end; out- of-range indices raise
an error.



- A scalar index returns a 1-by-1 matrix. If that element is a structural zero, the result is structurally empty (prints 00).
- A Slice (Python slice) or a list of indices returns a column vector, except that indexing a row vector gives a row vector.
- A matrix of indices (IM) returns a matrix of the same shape.
- A Sparsity pattern must have the same shape as the matrix; the result is the projection of the matrix onto that pattern (entries outside it are dropped).



Note that the result of a comparison such as A > 2 is a DM of zeros and
ones, not a boolean mask: indexing with it uses those values as linear
indices. Use a Sparsity pattern or the nz accessor for masking.



Parameters:
    ind1: True for one-based indices (MATLAB); Python always passes false
    rr: Linear index, Slice or list/matrix of indices
    sp: Sparsity pattern of the same shape as the matrix



::

  >>> A = DM([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]])
  >>> A[1], A[2], A[-1]
  (DM(4), DM(2), DM(6))
  >>> A[[0, 5]]
  DM([1, 6])
  >>> A[Sparsity.diag(2, 3)]
  DM(
  [[1, 00, 00],
   [00, 5, 00]])
  >>> S = DM(Sparsity.diag(2))
  >>> S[1]
  DM(00)
  >>> A.get(False, Slice(0, 2))
  DM([1, 4])
  >>> try: A[6]
  ... except Exception as e: print(\"error\")
  error
  





See also: set, get_nz, Sparsity::sub

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_2or

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/matrix_decl.hpp#L499
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/matrix_impl.hpp#L144-L159
";

