https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/matrix_impl.hpp#L89-L104
";

%feature("docstring")  casadi::Matrix< double >::set(const Matrix< double >
&m, bool ind1, const Slice &rr) "

Assign elements by a single (linear) index.

Backs the Python statements x[k] = v and x[sp] = v. Indices are linear,
column-major and zero-based (see get). The matrix is modified in place.



- ``m`` must be a scalar (assigned to every selected element) or have as many entries as there are indices (a vector of the other orientation is transposed). Any other shape raises an error.
- Assigning to a structural zero inserts a new nonzero; the sparsity pattern grows. Assigning a structurally empty value (e.g. DM(1, 1)) removes the entry instead.
- With a Sparsity index of the same shape, the entries at the pattern's positions receive ``m`` (a scalar) or the corresponding entries of ``m`` (a matrix).





Parameters:
    m: Value(s) to assign
    ind1: True for one-based indices (MATLAB); Python always passes false
    rr: Linear index, Slice or list/matrix of indices
    sp: Sparsity pattern of the same shape as the matrix



::

  >>> A = DM([[1, 2], [3, 4]])
  >>> A[1] = 10; A[[0, 3]] = [7, 8]
  >>> A
  DM(
  [[7, 2],
   [10, 8]])
  >>> A[Sparsity.diag(2)] = 0
  >>> A
  DM(
  [[0, 2],
   [10, 0]])
  >>> S = DM(Sparsity.diag(2))
  >>> S[1] = 5
  >>> S.nnz(), S[0, 1]
  (3, DM(00))
  >>> try: A[[0, 1]] = [1, 2, 3]
  ... except Exception as e: print(\"error\")
  error
  





See also: get, set_nz, erase

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_2ot

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/matrix_decl.hpp#L636
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/matrix_impl.hpp#L295-L311
";

