%feature("docstring")  casadi::Matrix< double >::get_nz(Matrix< double > &m,
bool ind1, const Slice &k) const "

Get structural nonzeros by their position in the nonzero storage.

Backs the Python expression x.nz[k]. Index k runs over the nonzeros in
column-major order, 0 <= k < nnz(); negative values count from the end and
out-of-range values raise an error. Structural zeros are skipped, so for a
dense matrix this coincides with linear indexing. The result is a column
vector shaped like the index (a row vector when indexing a row vector; a
matrix for a matrix index). Iterating over x.nz in Python yields the
nonzeros one by one.



Parameters:
    ind1: True for one-based indices (MATLAB); Python always passes false
    k: Nonzero index, Slice or list/matrix of indices



::

  >>> S = DM(Sparsity.diag(3)); S[0, 2] = 7
  >>> S.nz[:]
  DM([1, 1, 7, 1])
  >>> S.nz[2], S.nz[-1], len(S.nz)
  (DM(7), DM(1), 4)
  >>> x = SX.sym(\"x\", Sparsity.lower(2))
  >>> x.nz[[0, 2]]
  SX([x_0, x_2])
  >>> SX.sym(\"e\", 2, 0).nz[:].dim()
  '0x1'
  >>> try: S.nz[4]
  ... except Exception as e: print(\"error\")
  error
  





See also: set_nz, get, nonzeros

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_2ov

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/matrix_decl.hpp#L758
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/matrix_impl.hpp#L404-L413
";

