%feature("docstring")  casadi::MX::einstein(const MX &A, const MX &B, const
MX &C, const std::vector< casadi_int > &dim_a, const std::vector< casadi_int
> &dim_b, const std::vector< casadi_int > &dim_c, const std::vector<
casadi_int > &a, const std::vector< casadi_int > &b, const std::vector<
casadi_int > &c) "

Compute a dense tensor contraction in Einstein notation.

Computes C_c += A_a * B_b, where a, b and c are index labels in Einstein
notation: a label appearing in both ``a`` and ``b`` is summed over, the
remaining labels index the result. Tensors are passed as flat column vectors
in column-major (Fortran) order together with their dimensions. Labels are
encoded as strictly negative integers. For the matrix product C_ij = A_ik
B_kj one writes a = [-1, -3], b = [-3, -2], c = [-1, -2].



Parameters:
    A, B: Dense column vectors with product(dim_a), product(dim_b) entries
    C: Optional accumulator with product(dim_c) entries, added to the result; when omitted the result starts from zero
    dim_a, dim_b, dim_c: Tensor dimensions
    a, b, c: Negative labels, one per dimension



Returns:
    Column vector with product(dim_c) entries

Inconsistent dimensions or labels are not validated with a user- facing
message. Also available for SX and DM.



::

  >>> A = MX.sym(\"A\", 6)
  >>> B = MX.sym(\"B\", 6)
  >>> C = einstein(A, B, [2, 3], [3, 2], [2, 2], [-1, -3], [-3, -2], [-1, -2])
  >>> C.shape
  (4, 1)
  >>> f = Function(\"f\", [A, B], [C])
  >>> f(DM(range(1, 7)), DM(range(1, 7)))
  DM([22, 28, 49, 64])
  >>> reshape(DM(range(1, 7)), 2, 3) @ reshape(DM(range(1, 7)), 3, 2)
  DM(
  [[22, 49],
   [28, 64]])
  



Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_r5

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/mx.hpp#L2310
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/mx.cpp#L682-L688 ";

