%feature("docstring")  casadi::MX::low(const MX &v, const MX &p, const Dict
&options=Dict()) "

Find the interval of a sorted grid that contains each query value.

For each entry p_i of ``p``, returns the index k such that v[k] <= p_i <
v[k+1] for the ascending grid ``v``, clipped to [0, len(v)-2] so that values
outside the grid map to the first or last interval. This is the lookup step
of linear interpolation. The result has the shape of ``p`` and is piecewise
constant, so its derivative is zero.



Parameters:
    v: Dense ascending grid, vector
    p: Query values, dense vector
    options: \"lookup_mode\": \"linear\" (scan), \"binary\" (bisection), \"exact\" (assumes an equidistant grid and computes the index arithmetically) or \"auto\" (default: \"binary\" for grids longer than 100 entries, else \"linear\"). Any other key or mode raises an error.



::

  >>> g = MX.sym(\"g\", 4)
  >>> p = MX.sym(\"p\", 5)
  >>> f = Function(\"f\", [g, p], [low(g, p)])
  >>> f(DM([0, 1, 2, 3]), DM([-1, 0, 1.5, 3, 4]))
  DM([0, 0, 1, 2, 2])
  >>> jacobian(low(g, p), p).nnz()
  0
  >>> try:
  ...   low(g, p, {\"lookup_mode\": \"foo\"})
  ... except Exception as e:
  ...   print(\"Invalid lookup mode\" in str(e))
  True
  





See also: find, interpn_linear, interpolant

