%feature("docstring")  casadi::MX::get(MX &m, bool ind1, const Slice &rr)
const "

Get a submatrix by linear index (what x[k] does)

Backs single-index subscripting. Elements are addressed in column- major
order over all numel() entries, including structural zeros. Indices may be
negative (counted from the end); out-of-range indices raise an error. The
result is a new GetNonzeros node referencing the selected nonzeros of this
expression; a structural zero is returned as an empty-sparsity constant.
Accepted index types: integer, slice, list of integers, list of booleans
(mask), integer DM, Sparsity pattern of the same shape (returns the
projection on that pattern) and MX (a symbolic index into the nonzeros of a
dense expression, evaluated at run time with truncation and NaN for out-of-
range values).



Parameters:
    ind1: True for 1-based indices (MATLAB), false for 0-based
    rr: Element indices



::

  >>> X = MX.sym(\"X\", 3, 3)
  >>> X[4], X[-1], X[[0, 4]], X[1:3].shape
  (MX(X[4]), MX(X[8]), MX(X[:8:4]), (2, 1))
  >>> try:
  ...   X[9]
  ... except Exception as e:
  ...   print(\"Out of bounds\" in str(e))
  True
  >>> S = MX.sym(\"S\", Sparsity.lower(2))
  >>> S[2], S[2].nnz()
  (MX(00), 0)
  >>> X[Sparsity.lower(3)]
  MX(project(X))
  >>> k = MX.sym(\"k\")
  >>> v = MX.sym(\"v\", 4)
  >>> v[k], Function(\"f\", [v, k], [v[k]])(DM([10, 20, 30, 40]), 1.7)
  (MX(v[k]), DM(20))
  





See also: set, get_nz, nz

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_2qa

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/mx.hpp#L2000
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/mx.cpp#L183-L186 ";

%feature("docstring")  casadi::MX::get(MX &m, bool ind1, const Slice &rr,
const Slice &cc) const "

Get a submatrix by row and column index (what x[i, j] does)

Backs two-index subscripting. ``rr`` and ``cc`` select rows and columns
(integer, slice, list, integer DM); the result has shape len(rr)-by-len(cc)
and the corresponding part of the sparsity pattern. Out-of-range indices
raise an error. An MX index is only allowed for dense expressions and
selects at run time.



::

  >>> X = MX.sym(\"X\", 3, 3)
  >>> X[0, 1], X[1, :].shape, X[[0, 2], [0, 2]].shape
  (MX(X[3]), (1, 3), (2, 2))
  >>> S = MX.sym(\"S\", Sparsity.lower(3))
  >>> S[:, 0].sparsity(), S[0, 2].nnz()
  (Sparsity(3x1), 0)
  >>> k = MX.sym(\"k\")
  >>> X[k, 1]
