%feature("docstring")  casadi::MX::get_nz(MX &m, bool ind1, const Slice &kk)
const "

Get a set of nonzeros (what x.nz[k] does)

Indexes the nnz() nonzeros directly, skipping structural zeros, in column-
major order. Negative indices count from the end; out-of-range indices raise
an error. An MX index (or an inner/outer pair of indices) creates a
parametric lookup that is resolved at evaluation time; such a node has no
derivative with respect to the index. For dense expressions x[k] and x.nz[k]
coincide.



::

  >>> S = MX.sym(\"S\", Sparsity.lower(3))
  >>> S.nz[1], S.nz[:].shape, len(S.nz)
  (MX(S[1]), (6, 1), 6)
  >>> k = MX.sym(\"k\")
  >>> S.nz[k]
  MX(S[k])
  >>> MX.sym(\"x\", 2, 2).nz[[]].shape
  (0, 1)
  





