// File: classcasadi_1_1Opti.xml
%feature("docstring") casadi::Opti "

A simplified interface for NLP modeling/solving.

Opti collects decision variables, parameters, an objective and constraints
written in natural syntax, builds an nlpsol (or qpsol) instance from them
and gives numerical access to the solution. The bookkeeping of flattening
variables into one vector and constraints into bounds is hidden.

Workflow:

- Declare symbols with variable() and parameter(); they are plain MX symbols.
- State the problem with minimize() and subject_to().
- Pick a numerical back-end with solver().
- Give parameters a value with set_value(), optionally a guess with set_initial().
- Call solve(); it returns an OptiSol snapshot whose value() evaluates expressions.



Opti is stateful: every call above mutates the instance, and the problem is
rebuilt lazily at the next solve(). An OptiSol is a copy taken at the time
of solve(), so later changes to the Opti do not affect it. Only symbols
created by this instance may appear in its expressions; raw MX.sym symbols
or symbols of another Opti raise an error.

The constructor argument selects the problem class: \"nlp\" (default, solved
with nlpsol) or \"conic\" (quadratic programs and SOCPs, solved with qpsol).

An iteration callback can be registered with opti.callback(fh); fh is called
with the iteration number after every solver iteration, and
opti.debug.value() inside it returns the current iterate. Calling
opti.callback() without argument removes it.



::

  >>> opti = Opti()
  >>> x = opti.variable()
  >>> y = opti.variable()
  >>> opti.minimize((y-x**2)**2)
  >>> opti.subject_to(x**2+y**2==1)
  >>> opti.subject_to(x+y>=1)
  >>> opti.solver(\"ipopt\", {\"print_time\": False}, {\"print_level\": 0, \"sb\": \"yes\"})
  >>> sol = opti.solve()
  >>> round(sol.value(x), 4), round(sol.value(y), 4)
  (0.7862, 0.618)
  >>> round(sol.value(opti.f), 6)
  0.0
  



Parametric problems are re-solved by changing parameter values:



::

  >>> opti = Opti()
  >>> x = opti.variable(2)
  >>> p = opti.parameter()
  >>> opti.minimize(sumsqr(x-p))
  >>> opti.subject_to(1 <= (sum1(x) <= 2))
  >>> opti.solver(\"ipopt\", {\"print_time\": False}, {\"print_level\": 0, \"sb\": \"yes\"})
  >>> opti.set_value(p, 3)
  >>> sol = opti.solve()
  >>> round(sol.value(sum1(x)), 4)
  2.0
  >>> opti.set_value(p, 0)
  >>> sol = opti.solve()
  >>> round(sol.value(sum1(x)), 4)
  1.0
  



An iteration callback:



::

  >>> opti = Opti()
  >>> x = opti.variable()
  >>> opti.minimize((x-1)**2)
  >>> opti.solver(\"ipopt\", {\"print_time\": False}, {\"print_level\": 0, \"sb\": \"yes\"})
  >>> history = []
  >>> opti.callback(lambda i: history.append((i, round(opti.debug.value(x), 3))))
  >>> sol = opti.solve()
  >>> history
  [(0, 0.0), (1, 1.0)]
  >>> opti.callback()
  >>> sol = opti.solve()
  >>> len(history)
  2
  





See also: OptiSol, OptiAdvanced, nlpsol, qpsol



Authors: Joris Gillis, Erik Lambrechts, Joel Andersson

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_16

";

%feature("docstring")  casadi::Opti::subject_to(const MX &g, const Dict
&options=Dict()) "

Add constraints.

``g`` is a comparison expression built with ==, <=, >= (< and > behave as <=
and >=). Vector expressions give one constraint per element; both sides may
be vectors of the same shape or a scalar that is broadcast. Each call
appends to the constraint set.

Recognized forms and how they reach the solver:

- g(x,p) <= b(p), b(p) <= g(x,p), g(x,p) == b(p): one side free of decision variables becomes the bound (lbg/ubg) and the other side the constraint expression g.
- lb(p) <= (g(x,p) <= ub(p)): a chained inequality whose outer sides are free of decision variables becomes a double-bounded constraint lb <= g <= ub (one row). Both bounds may be vectors. In Python the inner comparison must be parenthesized, because the plain chain 0 <= x <= 1 asks for the truth value of an MX and raises an error; bounded() builds the same expression.
- g1(x,p) <= g2(x,p), g1(x,p) == g2(x,p): both sides depend on variables, giving g1-g2 <= 0 or g1-g2 == 0. A double-bounded constraint on a pure decision variable, such as -1 <= (x <= 1), is still passed as a constraint row; set the nlpsol option \"detect_simple_bounds\" in solver() to have it moved to the variable bounds lbx/ubx.



Errors: a constant comparison, an empty or fully sparse expression, a symbol
not declared by this instance, and dual variables all raise an error
immediately. A square matrix comparison A >= B is interpreted as a
semidefinite constraint, which \"nlp\" does not support: vectorize the
matrix with vec(A) for an elementwise constraint. An expression without
comparison operator is accepted but rejected at solve().



Parameters:
    g: Constraint expression, or a list of them (each added in turn)
    linear_scale: Scalar or same-shape matrix; the solver sees g divided by it
    options: Dict with optional entries \"meta\" (dict stored in the user_dict of the constraint) and \"stacktrace\"; any other key raises an error



::

  >>> opti = Opti()
  >>> x = opti.variable(3)
  >>> y = opti.variable()
  >>> p = opti.parameter()
  >>> opti.subject_to(x >= 0)
  >>> opti.subject_to(x[0] + x[1] == p)
  >>> opti.subject_to(-1 <= (y <= 1))
  >>> opti.subject_to([y >= x[2], y*x[0] >= 1])
  >>> opti.ng
  7
  >>> print(opti.lbg, opti.ubg)
  vertcat(zeros(3x1), opti..._p_1, -1, -inf, 1) vertcat(inf(3x1), opti..._p_1, 1, 0, inf)
  >>> try:
  ...   opti.subject_to(0 <= y <= 1)
  ... except Exception as e:
  ...   print(\"Can only determine truth value of a numeric MX\" in str(e))
  True
  >>> try:
  ...   opti.subject_to(MX(2) <= 3)
  ... except Exception as e:
  ...   print(str(e).splitlines()[-1])
  You passed a constant to `subject_to`. You need a symbol to form a constraint.
  >>> try:
  ...   opti.subject_to(MX.sym(\"w\") <= 3)
  ... except Exception as e:
  ...   print(\"MX symbol 'w' of shape 1x1, declared outside of Opti\" in str(e))
  True
  



Elementwise constraints on matrices need vec(); options attach metadata:



::

  >>> opti = Opti()
  >>> A = opti.variable(2, 2)
  >>> opti.subject_to(A >= 0)
  >>> opti.minimize(sumsqr(A))
  >>> opti.solver(\"ipopt\", {\"print_time\": False}, {\"print_level\": 0, \"sb\": \"yes\"})
  >>> try:
  ...   opti.solve()
  ... except Exception as e:
  ...   print(\"Psd constraints not implemented yet\" in str(e))
  True
  >>> opti.subject_to()
  >>> con = vec(A) >= 0
  >>> opti.subject_to(con, {\"meta\": {\"label\": \"nonneg\"}})
  >>> opti.ng, opti.user_dict(con)[\"label\"]
  (4, 'nonneg')
  





See also: bounded, subject_to(), dual, lbg, ubg, g

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_1b

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/optistack.hpp#L465
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/optistack.cpp#L122-L128
";

%feature("docstring")  casadi::Opti::subject_to() "

Clear all constraints.

Removes every constraint added so far, including their dual variables, so
the problem can be restated (typical when the constraint set changes between
solves). The objective, variables, parameters, values and initial guesses
are kept.



::

  >>> opti = Opti()
  >>> x = opti.variable()
  >>> con = x >= 1
  >>> opti.subject_to(con)
  >>> opti.minimize(x**2)
  >>> opti.ng
  1
  >>> opti.subject_to()
  >>> opti.ng
  0
  >>> opti.solver(\"ipopt\", {\"print_time\": False}, {\"print_level\": 0, \"sb\": \"yes\"})
  >>> sol = opti.solve()
  >>> try:
  ...   sol.value(opti.dual(con))
  ... except Exception as e:
  ...   print(str(e).splitlines()[-2])
  This expression has a dual for a constraint that is not given to Opti:
  



Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_2ms

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/optistack.hpp#L510
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/optistack.cpp#L146-L152
";

%feature("docstring")  casadi::Opti::set_initial(const MX &x, const DM &v) "

Set the initial guess of decision variables or dual variables.

The guess is used as x0 (and lam_g0 for opti.lam_g or opti.dual(con)) at the
next solve(); the default is 0. ``x`` may be a variable, a slice of one, or
a linear mapping of variables such as 10*x[0]; a nonlinear expression raises
an error. Parameters are refused with a hint to use set_value(). The list
form takes assignment expressions var==value, as returned by
value_variables() or initial(), which makes warm starting a one-liner.
Calling this method invalidates value() until the next solve().



Parameters:
    x: Variable (or linear mapping of variables) to initialize
    v: Numerical value; scalars are broadcast to the shape of ``x``



::

  >>> opti = Opti()
  >>> x = opti.variable(2)
  >>> p = opti.parameter()
  >>> opti.set_initial(x, 1)
  >>> opti.set_initial(10*x[0], 2)
  >>> print(opti.initial())
  [MX(([0.2, 1]==opti..._x_1))]
  >>> try:
  ...   opti.set_initial(p, 1)
  ... except Exception as e:
  ...   print(str(e).splitlines()[-1])
  You cannot set an initial value for a parameter. Did you mean 'set_value'?
  >>> try:
  ...   opti.set_initial(x[0]*x[1], 1)
  ... except Exception as e:
  ...   print(\"Use symbols or simple mappings of symbols\" in str(e))
  True
  



Warm start from a previous solution:



::

  >>> opti = Opti()
  >>> x = opti.variable()
  >>> y = opti.variable()
  >>> opti.minimize((y-x**2)**2)
  >>> opti.subject_to(x**2+y**2==1)
  >>> opti.subject_to(x+y>=1)
  >>> opti.solver(\"ipopt\", {\"print_time\": False}, {\"print_level\": 0, \"sb\": \"yes\"})
  >>> sol = opti.solve()
  >>> opti.set_initial(sol.value_variables())
  >>> opti.set_initial(opti.lam_g, sol.value(opti.lam_g))
  >>> opti.solve().stats()[\"iter_count\"] < sol.stats()[\"iter_count\"]
  True
  





See also: set_value, initial, value_variables

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_2mt

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/optistack.hpp#L646
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/optistack.cpp#L165-L171
";

%feature("docstring")  casadi::Opti::set_value(const MX &x, const DM &v) "

Set the value of a parameter.

Every parameter that appears in the problem must have a value before
solve(); the value stays until changed, so re-solving with new data only
needs set_value() and solve(). ``x`` may be a parameter, a slice, or a
linear mapping of parameters. Decision variables are refused with a hint to
use set_initial(). The list form accepts assignment expressions par==value,
as returned by value_parameters(). Calling this method invalidates value()
of variables until the next solve().



Parameters:
    x: Parameter (or linear mapping of parameters)
    v: Numerical value; scalars are broadcast



::

  >>> opti = Opti()
  >>> x = opti.variable()
  >>> p = opti.parameter(2)
  >>> opti.set_value(p, [1, 2])
  >>> opti.set_value(p[1], 5)
  >>> opti.value(p)
  array([1., 5.])
  >>> try:
  ...   opti.set_value(x, 1)
  ... except Exception as e:
  ...   print(str(e).splitlines()[-1])
  You cannot set a value for a variable. Did you mean 'set_initial'?
  >>> opti.set_value([p==7])
  >>> opti.value(p)
  array([7., 7.])
  





See also: set_initial, value_parameters

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_1d

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/optistack.hpp#L694
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/optistack.cpp#L181-L187
";

%feature("docstring")  casadi::Opti::value(const MX &x, const std::vector<
MX > &values=std::vector< MX >()) const "

Evaluate an expression at the current values.

Variables take the latest solver result, parameters their set_value(), dual
variables the latest multipliers. Before the first solve(), or after
set_initial()/set_value() changed the state, an expression involving
variables raises an error unless every variable in it is overruled through
``values``; parameter-only expressions always work. After a failed solve()
the latest iterate is returned (the same holds for opti.debug.value()). A
variable that does not appear in the problem raises an error.



Parameters:
    x: Expression in the symbols of this instance (an MX). A DM is returned as is. SX expressions are not supported: a NaN matrix of the same sparsity is returned.
    values: Optional list of assignments var==value (e.g. opti.initial()) that overrule the stored values for this evaluation only



Returns:
    The numerical value. In Python a scalar becomes a float, a dense matrix a numpy array and a sparse matrix a scipy sparse matrix.



::

  >>> opti = Opti()
  >>> x = opti.variable()
  >>> p = opti.parameter()
  >>> opti.minimize((x-p)**2)
  >>> opti.set_value(p, 3)
  >>> opti.value(2*p)
  6.0
  >>> try:
  ...   opti.value(x)
  ... except Exception as e:
  ...   print(str(e).splitlines()[-1])
  This action is forbidden since you have not solved the Opti stack yet (with calling 'solve').
  >>> opti.value(x+p, [x==1]), opti.value(x**2, opti.initial())
  (4.0, 0.0)
  >>> opti.solver(\"ipopt\", {\"print_time\": False}, {\"print_level\": 0, \"sb\": \"yes\"})
  >>> sol = opti.solve()
  >>> round(opti.value(x), 4), round(sol.value(x), 4), sol.value(x+p, [p==10])
  (3.0, 3.0, 13.0)
  >>> type(sol.value(vertcat(x, p))).__name__, sol.value(DM([1, 2]))
  ('ndarray', array([1., 2.]))
  





See also: initial, value_variables, OptiSol::value

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_2mv

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/optistack.hpp#L922
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/optistack.cpp#L229-L235
";

%feature("docstring")  casadi::Opti::to_function(const std::string &name,
const std::vector< MX > &args, const std::vector< MX > &res, const Dict
&opts=Dict()) "

Create a CasADi Function from the Opti solver.

Packs bounds evaluation, the solver call and the evaluation of ``res`` into
one Function, which is much cheaper to call repeatedly than
set_value/solve/value. Inputs corresponding to parameters supply their
values; inputs corresponding to variables or dual variables supply the
initial guess; anything not listed keeps the value stored in the Opti at the
time of the call. Outputs are evaluated at the solution.

A solver failure does not raise by default: the last iterate is returned and
the Function's stats() report success False. Pass \"error_on_fail\": True in
the plugin_options of solver() to raise instead. Function options in
``opts`` are applied to the result; the entry \"helper_options\" is applied
to the internal evaluation function.



Parameters:
    name: Name of the resulting Function
    args: List of parameters, decision variables and dual variables (or vertcat of them); each must be purely symbolic and independent, else an error is raised
    res: List of expressions evaluated at the solution
    name_in: Optional input names (same length as ``args``)
    name_out: Optional output names (same length as ``res``)
    opts: Function options



::

  >>> opti = Opti()
  >>> x = opti.variable()
  >>> y = opti.variable()
  >>> p = opti.parameter()
  >>> opti.minimize((x-p)**2 + (y-x)**2)
  >>> opti.subject_to(x >= 0)
  >>> opti.solver(\"ipopt\", {\"print_time\": False}, {\"print_level\": 0, \"sb\": \"yes\"})
  >>> F = opti.to_function(\"F\", [p, x], [vertcat(x, y), opti.lam_g],
  ...                      [\"p\", \"x0\"], [\"xy\", \"lam\"])
  >>> F
  Function(F:(p,x0)->(xy[2],lam) MXFunction)
  >>> r = F(p=2, x0=1)
  >>> r[\"xy\"], round(abs(float(r[\"lam\"])), 4)
  (DM([2, 2]), 0.0)
  >>> F.stats()[\"success\"]
  True
  >>> F = opti.to_function(\"F\", {\"p\": p, \"x\": x}, [\"p\"], [\"x\"])
  >>> F(3)
  DM(3)
  >>> try:
  ...   opti.to_function(\"F\", [p+1], [x])
  ... except Exception as e:
  ...   print(str(e).splitlines()[-1])
  Argument 0 is not purely symbolic.
  





See also: nlpsol

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_1j

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/optistack.hpp#L1464
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/optistack.cpp#L435-L439
";

