%feature("docstring")  casadi::Opti::set_initial(const MX &x, const DM &v) "

Set the initial guess of decision variables or dual variables.

The guess is used as x0 (and lam_g0 for opti.lam_g or opti.dual(con)) at the
next solve(); the default is 0. ``x`` may be a variable, a slice of one, or
a linear mapping of variables such as 10*x[0]; a nonlinear expression raises
an error. Parameters are refused with a hint to use set_value(). The list
form takes assignment expressions var==value, as returned by
value_variables() or initial(), which makes warm starting a one-liner.
Calling this method invalidates value() until the next solve().



Parameters:
    x: Variable (or linear mapping of variables) to initialize
    v: Numerical value; scalars are broadcast to the shape of ``x``



::

  >>> opti = Opti()
  >>> x = opti.variable(2)
  >>> p = opti.parameter()
  >>> opti.set_initial(x, 1)
  >>> opti.set_initial(10*x[0], 2)
  >>> print(opti.initial())
  [MX(([0.2, 1]==opti..._x_1))]
  >>> try:
  ...   opti.set_initial(p, 1)
  ... except Exception as e:
  ...   print(str(e).splitlines()[-1])
  You cannot set an initial value for a parameter. Did you mean 'set_value'?
  >>> try:
  ...   opti.set_initial(x[0]*x[1], 1)
  ... except Exception as e:
  ...   print(\"Use symbols or simple mappings of symbols\" in str(e))
  True
  



Warm start from a previous solution:



::

  >>> opti = Opti()
  >>> x = opti.variable()
  >>> y = opti.variable()
  >>> opti.minimize((y-x**2)**2)
  >>> opti.subject_to(x**2+y**2==1)
  >>> opti.subject_to(x+y>=1)
  >>> opti.solver(\"ipopt\", {\"print_time\": False}, {\"print_level\": 0, \"sb\": \"yes\"})
  >>> sol = opti.solve()
  >>> opti.set_initial(sol.value_variables())
  >>> opti.set_initial(opti.lam_g, sol.value(opti.lam_g))
  >>> opti.solve().stats()[\"iter_count\"] < sol.stats()[\"iter_count\"]
  True
  





See also: set_value, initial, value_variables

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_2mt

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/optistack.hpp#L646
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/optistack.cpp#L165-L171
";

%feature("docstring")  casadi::Opti::set_value(const MX &x, const DM &v) "

Set the value of a parameter.

Every parameter that appears in the problem must have a value before
solve(); the value stays until changed, so re-solving with new data only
needs set_value() and solve(). ``x`` may be a parameter, a slice, or a
linear mapping of parameters. Decision variables are refused with a hint to
use set_initial(). The list form accepts assignment expressions par==value,
as returned by value_parameters(). Calling this method invalidates value()
of variables until the next solve().



Parameters:
    x: Parameter (or linear mapping of parameters)
    v: Numerical value; scalars are broadcast



::

  >>> opti = Opti()
  >>> x = opti.variable()
  >>> p = opti.parameter(2)
  >>> opti.set_value(p, [1, 2])
  >>> opti.set_value(p[1], 5)
  >>> opti.value(p)
  array([1., 5.])
  >>> try:
  ...   opti.set_value(x, 1)
  ... except Exception as e:
  ...   print(str(e).splitlines()[-1])
  You cannot set a value for a variable. Did you mean 'set_initial'?
  >>> opti.set_value([p==7])
  >>> opti.value(p)
  array([7., 7.])
  





See also: set_initial, value_parameters

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_1d

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/optistack.hpp#L694
