%feature("docstring")  casadi::Sparsity::ldl(std::vector< casadi_int > &p,
bool amd=true) const "

Symbolic LDL factorization of a symmetric pattern.

Computes the pattern of the factor in A[p, p] = L*D*L^T, where L is unit
lower triangular. Returns the pattern of the strictly upper triangular L^T
(the unit diagonal is not stored) and the ordering p. A non-symmetric
pattern raises an error. The numerical counterpart is ldl() applied to a
matrix.



Parameters:
    amd: If true (default), a fill-reducing AMD ordering is computed and returned in p; if false, p is the identity



Returns:
    Pattern of L^T and the permutation vector p



::

  >>> Lt, p = Sparsity.banded(4, 1).ldl(False)
  >>> DM(Lt, 1), p
  (DM(
  [[00, 1, 00, 00],
   [00, 00, 1, 00],
   [00, 00, 00, 1],
   [00, 00, 00, 00]]), [0, 1, 2, 3])
  >>> A = Sparsity.nonzeros(4, 4, [0, 3, 5, 10, 12, 15])
  >>> Lt, p = A.ldl()
  >>> Lt.nnz(), p
  (1, [1, 2, 0, 3])
  >>> try: Sparsity.upper(3).ldl()
  ... except Exception as e: print(\"not symmetric\")
  not symmetric
  



The implementation is a modified version of LDL Copyright(c) Timothy A.
Davis, 2005-2013 Licensed as a derivative work under the GNU LGPL



See also: amd, etree, qr_sparse

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_d3

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/sparsity.hpp#L3299
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/sparsity.cpp#L622-L652
";

%feature("docstring")  casadi::Sparsity::qr_sparse(Sparsity &V, Sparsity &R,
std::vector< casadi_int > &prinv, std::vector< casadi_int > &pc, bool
amd=true) const "

Symbolic QR factorization.

Computes the patterns of V (the Householder vectors, a compact
representation of Q) and R for the factorization of this pattern with its
columns permuted by pc, plus the row permutation prinv used by the numerical
factorization (qr_sparse on a matrix) and solution. V and R may have more
rows than the pattern (the matrix is extended with zero rows when it has
fewer rows than columns in the structural sense).



Parameters:
    amd: If true (default), the columns are reordered with the AMD ordering of A^T*A, returned in pc; if false, pc is the identity



Returns:
    V, R, prinv, pc



::

  >>> V, R, prinv, pc = Sparsity.dense(3, 2).qr_sparse()
  >>> V, R, prinv, pc
  (Sparsity(3x2,5nz), Sparsity(3x2,3nz), [0, 1, 2], [0, 1])
  >>> V, R, prinv, pc = Sparsity.banded(4, 1).qr_sparse(False)
  >>> V, R, prinv, pc
  (Sparsity(4x4,7nz), Sparsity(4x4,9nz), [0, 1, 2, 3], [0, 1, 2, 3])
  



The implementation is a modified version of CSparse Copyright(c) Timothy A.
Davis, 2006-2009 Licensed as a derivative work under the GNU LGPL



See also: ldl, amd, etree

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_d4

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/sparsity.hpp#L3344
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/sparsity.cpp#L655-L696
";

%feature("docstring")  casadi::Sparsity::dfs(casadi_int j, casadi_int top,
std::vector< casadi_int > &SWIG_INOUT1(xi), std::vector< casadi_int >
&SWIG_INOUT2(pstack), const std::vector< casadi_int > &pinv, std::vector<
bool > &SWIG_INOUT3(marked)) const "

Depth-first search on the adjacency graph of the sparsity.

Low-level building block of scc (cs_dfs in CSparse). Starting from column
``j``, it visits the columns reachable through the nonzeros and writes them,
in reverse finishing order, into ``xi`` below position ``top``. See Direct
Methods for Sparse Linear Systems by Davis (2006).



Parameters:
    j: Start node (column)
    top: Current top of the output stack in ``xi``
    xi: Output stack, length at least size2(); the visited nodes are stored in xi[new_top:top]
    pstack: Work vector, length at least size2()
    pinv: Inverse row permutation, or an empty vector for none
    marked: Visited flags per node, updated in place



Returns:
    The new top of the stack, with the updated xi, pstack and marked vectors



::

  >>> Sparsity.banded(3, 1).dfs(0, 3, [0, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0], [], [False] * 3)
  (0, [0, 1, 2], [1, 4, 5], [True, True, True])
  





See also: scc

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_d5

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/sparsity.hpp#L3382
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/sparsity.cpp#L698-L703
";

%feature("docstring")  casadi::Sparsity::scc(std::vector< casadi_int >
&index, std::vector< casadi_int > &offset) const "

Strongly connected components of the directed graph of a square pattern.

Node j is the j-th row/column and nonzero (i, j) is an edge from j to i. See
Direct Methods for Sparse Linear Systems by Davis (2006).



Returns:
    The number of components n, the node indices grouped by component, and the offsets (length n+1): component i consists of the nodes index[offset[i]],..., index[offset[i+1]-1], in increasing order.

The components are ordered so that the symmetric permutation A[index, index]
is block upper triangular with n diagonal blocks; for a symmetric pattern it
is block diagonal, with the block boundaries given by offset.

The implementation is a modified version of cs_scc in CSparse Copyright(c)
Timothy A. Davis, 2006-2009 Licensed as a derivative work under the GNU LGPL



::

  >>> A = Sparsity.nonzeros(4, 4, [0, 2, 5, 7, 10, 13, 15])
  >>> DM(A, 1)
  DM(
  [[1, 00, 00, 00],
   [00, 1, 00, 1],
   [1, 00, 1, 00],
   [00, 1, 00, 1]])
  >>> n, index, offset = A.scc()
  >>> n, index, offset
  (3, [2, 0, 1, 3], [0, 1, 2, 4])
  >>> DM(A, 1)[index, index]
  DM(
  [[1, 1, 00, 00],
   [00, 1, 00, 00],
   [00, 00, 1, 1],
   [00, 00, 1, 1]])
  >>> Sparsity.diag(3).scc(), Sparsity.dense(3, 3).scc(), Sparsity(0, 0).scc()
  ((3, [0, 1, 2], [0, 1, 2, 3]), (1, [0, 1, 2], [0, 3]), (0, [], [0]))
  





See also: btf, dfs

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_d6

Doc source:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/sparsity.hpp#L3457
Implementation:
https://github.com/casadi/casadi/blob/main/casadi/core/sparsity.cpp#L705-L707
";

%feature("docstring")  casadi::Sparsity::btf(std::vector< casadi_int >
&rowperm, std::vector< casadi_int > &colperm, std::vector< casadi_int >
&rowblock, std::vector< casadi_int > &colblock, std::vector< casadi_int >
&coarse_rowblock, std::vector< casadi_int > &coarse_colblock) const "

Block triangular form (BTF) through the Dulmage-Mendelsohn decomposition.

Computes a row permutation and a column permutation that bring the pattern
into block upper triangular form, see Direct Methods for Sparse Linear
Systems by Davis (2006). The pattern may be rectangular.



Returns:
    the number of fine blocks nb rowperm, colperm: row and column permutation vectors, so that A[rowperm, colperm] is in block upper triangular form rowblock, colblock (length nb+1): block k spans rows rowblock[k] to rowblock[k+1]-1 and columns colblock[k] to colblock[k+1]-1 of the permuted matrix coarse_rowblock, coarse_colblock (length 5): boundaries of the coarse decomposition into the underdetermined, square (well-determined) and overdetermined parts; coarse_colblock[3] is the structural rank (sprank) the number of fine blocks nb rowperm, colperm: row and column permutation vectors, so that A[rowperm, colperm] is in block upper triangular form rowblock, colblock (length nb+1): block k spans rows rowblock[k] to rowblock[k+1]-1 and columns colblock[k] to colblock[k+1]-1 of the permuted matrix coarse_rowblock, coarse_colblock (length 5): boundaries of the coarse decomposition into the underdetermined, square (well-determined) and overdetermined parts; coarse_colblock[3] is the structural rank (sprank)

Only structure is used: the distance of entries to the diagonal plays no
role, so a randomly permuted block-diagonal matrix is not necessarily
brought back to block diagonal form, and the row and column permutations
differ in general, which destroys symmetry. The result is cached in the
pattern.

The implementation is a modified version of cs_dmperm in CSparse
Copyright(c) Timothy A. Davis, 2006-2009 Licensed as a derivative work under
the GNU LGPL



::

  >>> B = Sparsity.triplet(3, 3, [0, 0, 1, 2], [0, 2, 1, 2])
  >>> DM(B, 1)
  DM(
  [[1, 00, 1],
   [00, 1, 00],
   [00, 00, 1]])
  >>> nb, rowperm, colperm, rowblock, colblock, crb, ccb = B.btf()
  >>> nb, rowperm, colperm, rowblock, colblock
  (3, [2, 0, 1], [2, 0, 1], [0, 1, 2, 3], [0, 1, 2, 3])
  >>> crb, ccb
  ([0, 0, 0, 3, 3], [0, 0, 3, 3, 3])
  >>> DM(B, 1)[rowperm, colperm]
  DM(
  [[1, 00, 00],
   [1, 1, 00],
   [00, 00, 1]])
  >>> sprank(Sparsity.nonzeros(3, 3, [0, 1, 2])), sprank(Sparsity.diag(3))
  (1, 3)
  





See also: scc, sprank, is_singular

Extra doc: https://github.com/casadi/casadi/wiki/L_d7

