%feature("docstring")  casadi::integrator(const std::string &name, const
std::string &solver, const SXDict &dae, const Dict &opts=Dict()) "



Create an ODE/DAE integrator Solves an initial value problem (IVP) coupled
to a terminal value problem with differential equation given as an implicit
ODE coupled to an algebraic equation and a set of quadratures:



::

  Initial conditions at t=t0
  x(t0)  = x0
  q(t0)  = 0
  
  Forward integration from t=t0 to t=tf
  der(x) = fx(x, z, p, u, t)                  Forward ODE
  0 = fz(x, z, p, u, t)                  Forward algebraic equations
  der(q) = fq(x, z, p, u, t)                  Forward quadratures
  
  Terminal conditions at t=tf
  rx(tf)  = rx0
  rq(tf)  = 0
  
  Backward integration from t=tf to t=t0
  der(rx) = gx(rx, rz, rp, x, z, p, t)        Backward ODE
  0 = gz(rx, rz, rp, x, z, p, t)        Backward algebraic equations
  der(rq) = gq(rx, rz, rp, x, z, p, t)        Backward quadratures
  
  where we assume that both the forward and backwards integrations are index-1
  (i.e. dfz/dz, dgz/drz are invertible) and furthermore that
  gx, gz and gq have a linear dependency on rx, rz and rp.



The DAE is given as a dictionary with the fields \"x\" (differential state),
\"ode\" (its right-hand side, same size as \"x\") and optionally \"t\"
(time, scalar), \"z\" (algebraic variables) with \"alg\" (residual, same
size as \"z\"), \"p\" (parameters), \"u\" (controls, piecewise constant on
the output grid), \"quad\" (quadrature right-hand side) and \"zero\" (event
indicators, experimental). Every entry must be a dense vector; row vectors
are flattened to columns. Any other field name is an error, as is a symbol
in an output that is not an input. The DAE can also be given as a Function
with the full input scheme t, x, z, p, u and output scheme ode, alg, quad,
zero (see dyn_in and dyn_out), empty where unused. The pure ODE integrators
(cvodes, rk, erk, rosenbrock) reject a problem with \"z\".

Time grid: integrator(name, solver, dae, opts) integrates from t0 = 0 to tf
= 1; integrator(name, solver, dae, t0, tf, opts) to a single output time tf;
and integrator(name, solver, dae, t0, tout, opts) returns the solution at
every time in the list ``tout``. The grid must be increasing but need not
contain t0; including it gives the initial state as the first output column.
t0 > tf integrates backwards in time. The legacy options \"t0\", \"tf\",
\"grid\" and \"output_t0\" are deprecated and emit a warning.

The returned Function has inputs x0, z0, p, u, adj_xf, adj_zf, adj_qf (see
integrator_in) and outputs xf, zf, qf, adj_x0, adj_z0, adj_p, adj_u (see
integrator_out). With N output times, x0, z0 and p are column vectors, u has
one column per output time (the control on the interval ending at that time;
a single column is broadcast), and xf, zf, qf have one column per output
time. z0 is the initial guess for the algebraic variables; it must be
consistent for the Sundials solvers to converge. Omitted inputs are zero.
The adj_* inputs and outputs are empty unless option \"nadj\" is positive:
then adj_xf, adj_zf and adj_qf take one adjoint seed per output time and
adj_x0, adj_p and adj_u return the corresponding adjoint sensitivities, i.e.
adj_x0 is the gradient of sum(adj_xf .* xf) + sum(adj_qf .* qf) with respect
to x0. Option \"nfwd\" adds, in the same way, forward sensitivity columns to
every input and output. Without these options derivatives are obtained by
embedding the call in an MX expression and differentiating (sensitivity
equations are created on demand).

Options common to all plugins (the plugin options follow in the tables
below):

- \"nfwd\", \"nadj\": number of forward and adjoint sensitivity directions built into the Function (default 0).
- \"expand\": convert an MX DAE to SX before creating derivative functions (default false).
- \"print_stats\": print integrator statistics after each call; stats() returns them.
- \"transition\", \"max_event_iter\", \"max_events\", \"event_tol\": event handling for a DAE with \"zero\" indicators (experimental, see event_in and event_out).
- \"augmented_options\": options passed to the integrator created for sensitivities.
- \"print_time\", \"verbose\", \"monitor\", \"common_options\", \"specific_options\": diagnostics and options of the generated derivative functions, as for nlpsol.





::

  >>> x = SX.sym(\"x\"); p = SX.sym(\"p\")
  >>> dae = {\"x\": x, \"p\": p, \"ode\": -p*x, \"quad\": x}
  >>> F = integrator(\"F\", \"cvodes\", dae)
  >>> print(F)
  F:(x0,z0[0],p,u[0],adj_xf[],adj_zf[],adj_qf[])->(xf,zf[0],qf,adj_x0[],...,adj_u[]) ...
  >>> r = F(x0=1, p=1)
  >>> round(float(r[\"xf\"]), 4), round(float(r[\"qf\"]), 4)
  (0.3679, 0.6321)
  >>> F = integrator(\"F\", \"rk\", dae, 0, [1, 2, 3])
  >>> np.round(F(x0=1, p=1)[\"xf\"].full(), 4)
  array([[0.3679, 0.1353, 0.0498]])
  >>> xf, zf, qf, adj_x0, adj_z0, adj_p, adj_u = F(1, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0)
  >>> xf.shape, zf.shape, adj_x0.shape
  ((1, 3), (0, 3), (0, 0))
  





::

  >>> x = SX.sym(\"x\"); z = SX.sym(\"z\"); u = SX.sym(\"u\")
  >>> F = integrator(\"F\", \"idas\", {\"x\": x, \"z\": z, \"ode\": -z, \"alg\": z**2 - x})
  >>> r = F(x0=1, z0=1)
  >>> round(float(r[\"xf\"]), 3), round(float(r[\"zf\"]), 3)
  (0.25, 0.5)
  >>> round(float(F(x0=1, z0=-1)[\"zf\"]), 3)
  -1.5
  >>> F = integrator(\"F\", \"rk\", {\"x\": x, \"u\": u, \"ode\": u}, 0, [1, 2, 3])
  >>> F(x0=0, u=[1, 2, 3])[\"xf\"], F(x0=0, u=1)[\"xf\"]
  (DM([[1, 3, 6]]), DM([[1, 2, 3]]))
  >>> try:
  ...     integrator(\"F\", \"cvodes\", {\"x\": x, \"z\": z, \"ode\": -z, \"alg\": z - x})
  ... except RuntimeError as e:
  ...     print(\"does not support algebraic variables\" in str(e))
  True
  >>> try:
  ...     integrator(\"F\", \"cvodes\", {\"x\": x, \"ode\": vertcat(x, x)})
  ... except RuntimeError as e:
  ...     print(\"Dimension mismatch for 'ode'\" in str(e))
  True
  





::

  >>> x = SX.sym(\"x\"); p = SX.sym(\"p\")
  >>> dae = {\"x\": x, \"p\": p, \"ode\": -p*x, \"quad\": x}
  >>> F = integrator(\"F\", \"cvodes\", dae, {\"nadj\": 1})
  >>> print(F)
  F:(x0,z0[0],p,u[0],adj_xf,adj_zf[0],adj_qf)->(xf,zf[0],qf,adj_x0,...,adj_p,adj_u[0]) ...
  >>> r = F(x0=1, p=1, adj_xf=1)
  >>> round(float(r[\"adj_x0\"]), 4), round(float(r[\"adj_p\"]), 4)
  (0.3679, -0.3679)
  >>> F = integrator(\"F\", \"cvodes\", dae)
  >>> pm = MX.sym(\"p\")
  >>> J = Function(\"J\", [pm], [jacobian(F(x0=1, p=pm)[\"xf\"], pm)])
  >>> round(float(J(1)), 4)
  -0.3679
  



