%feature("docstring")  casadi::nlpsol(const std::string &name, const
std::string &solver, const SXDict &nlp, const Dict &opts=Dict()) "



Create an NLP solver

Returns a Function that solves the parametric nonlinear program (NLP)

::

  min          F(x, p)
  x
  
  subject to
  LBX <=   x    <= UBX
  LBG <= G(x, p) <= UBG
  p  == P
  
  nx: number of decision variables
  ng: number of constraints
  np: number of parameters



The problem is given as a dictionary with fields \"x\" (decision variables,
a dense vector), \"f\" (scalar objective), and optionally \"p\" (parameters,
dense vector) and \"g\" (constraint expression, dense vector). All entries
must be of one symbolic type (SX or MX). A missing \"f\" is taken as 0 and a
missing \"g\" as an empty vector; any other field name is an error, as is a
symbol in \"f\" or \"g\" that is not part of \"x\" or \"p\". The problem can
also be given as a Function with inputs \"x\", \"p\" and outputs \"f\",
\"g\" (for instance one created with external), as the name of a shared
library or a \".c\" file (compiled on the fly with clang) containing such a
function named \"nlp\", as an Importer, or as an NlpBuilder (an .nl file).

The returned Function has inputs x0, p, lbx, ubx, lbg, ubg, lam_x0, lam_g0
(see nlpsol_in) and outputs x, f, g, lam_x, lam_g, lam_p (see nlpsol_out).
Call it with keyword arguments (returns a dictionary) or positionally with
all 8 inputs (returns a tuple). Omitted inputs take their defaults: -inf for
lbx and lbg, +inf for ubx and ubg, 0 otherwise (see nlpsol_default_in). A
scalar passed for a vector input is broadcast to every entry. An input with
lb > ub, lb = +inf or ub = -inf raises an \"Ill-posed problem\" error.

Multiplier convention: lam_x and lam_g are the Lagrange multipliers of the
bound constraints on x and g; a multiplier is negative when the lower bound
is active and positive when the upper bound is active, such that grad_x f +
jac_x(g)' lam_g + lam_x = 0 at the solution. lam_p is the multiplier of p ==
P, i.e. minus the gradient of the optimal objective with respect to p.
lam_x0 and lam_g0 are only used by solvers that support warm starts.

When the solver does not converge, the result still holds the last iterate
and stats() reports \"success\" False and the plugin-specific
\"return_status\"; with option \"error_on_fail\" True an exception is raised
instead (default False for nlpsol).

Options common to all plugins (the plugin options follow in the tables
below):

- \"expand\": convert an MX problem to SX before creating the derivative functions (default false). Usually faster evaluation; fails on nodes without SX support.
- \"print_time\": print a timing table after each call (default true for nlpsol).
- \"error_on_fail\": raise on an unsuccessful solve (default false).
- \"calc_lam_x\", \"calc_lam_p\", \"calc_f\", \"calc_g\": recompute lam_x (from the stationarity condition), lam_p, f and g in the base class from the solution returned by the plugin (defaults false, true, false, false). \"calc_multipliers\" sets calc_lam_x and calc_lam_p.
- \"bound_consistency\": after the solve, clip x and g to their bounds and set each multiplier to the sign of its active bound, or 0 when both bounds are infinite (default false).
- \"discrete\": list of nx booleans marking integer variables; requires a plugin with integer support, otherwise an error at construction.
- \"equality\": list of ng booleans hinting which constraints have equal bounds.
- \"warn_initial_bounds\": warn when x0 violates lbx or ubx (default false).
- \"iteration_callback\": a Function (typically a Callback) with the nlpsol output scheme as inputs, called every \"iteration_callback_step\" iterations with the current iterate.
- \"detect_simple_bounds\": move entries of g that are bounds on a single variable into the simple bounds lbx/ubx (default false).
- \"verbose\", \"monitor\", \"show_eval_warnings\", \"common_options\", \"specific_options\": control diagnostics and the options of the generated derivative functions.



Plugin options are passed in a nested dictionary under the plugin name (e.g.
{\"ipopt\": {\"print_level\": 0}}) or dotted (\"ipopt.print_level\"). The
call to the returned Function can be embedded in an MX expression and
differentiated: the sensitivities of the primal-dual solution with respect
to any input follow from the KKT conditions.



::

  >>> x = SX.sym(\"x\"); y = SX.sym(\"y\"); p = SX.sym(\"p\")
  >>> nlp = {\"x\": vertcat(x, y), \"p\": p, \"f\": (1 - x)**2 + p*(y - x**2)**2, \"g\": x + y}
  >>> opts = {\"ipopt.print_level\": 0, \"ipopt.sb\": \"yes\", \"print_time\": False}
  >>> S = nlpsol(\"S\", \"ipopt\", nlp, opts)
  >>> print(S)
  S:(x0[2],p,lbx[2],ubx[2],lbg,ubg,lam_x0[2],lam_g0)->(x[2],f,g,lam_x[2],lam_g,lam_p) ...
  >>> sol = S(x0=[2.5, 3.0], p=100, lbg=-1, ubg=1)
  >>> round(float(sol[\"f\"]), 4), np.round(sol[\"x\"].full().ravel(), 4)
  (0.1456, array([0.6188, 0.3812]))
  >>> S.stats()[\"success\"], S.stats()[\"return_status\"]
  (True, 'Solve_Succeeded')
  >>> x_opt, f_opt, g_opt, lam_x, lam_g, lam_p = S([2.5, 3.0], 100, -inf, inf, -1, 1, 0, 0)
  >>> round(float(f_opt), 4), round(float(lam_g), 4)
  (0.1456, 0.3407)
  >>> S(p=100)[\"x\"]
  DM([1, 1])
  





::

  >>> x = SX.sym(\"x\"); p = SX.sym(\"p\")
  >>> opts = {\"ipopt.print_level\": 0, \"ipopt.sb\": \"yes\", \"print_time\": False}
  >>> S = nlpsol(\"S\", \"ipopt\", {\"x\": x, \"p\": p, \"f\": (x - p)**2 + 3*p, \"g\": 2*x}, opts)
  >>> sol = S(p=1, lbg=4)
  >>> round(float(sol[\"x\"]), 4), round(float(sol[\"lam_g\"]), 4)
  (2.0, -1.0)
  >>> round(float(sol[\"lam_p\"]), 4)
  -1.0
  >>> round(float(S(p=1, ubx=0)[\"lam_x\"]), 4)
  2.0
  >>> pm = MX.sym(\"p\")
  >>> dxdp = Function(\"dxdp\", [pm], [jacobian(S(p=pm)[\"x\"], pm)])
  >>> dxdp(5)
  DM(1)
  





::

  >>> x = SX.sym(\"x\")
  >>> opts = {\"ipopt.print_level\": 0, \"ipopt.sb\": \"yes\", \"print_time\": False}
  >>> S = nlpsol(\"S\", \"ipopt\", {\"x\": x, \"f\": x, \"g\": x}, opts)
  >>> sol = S(lbx=0, ubx=1, lbg=2, ubg=3)
  >>> S.stats()[\"success\"], S.stats()[\"return_status\"]
  (False, 'Infeasible_Problem_Detected')
  >>> S = nlpsol(\"S\", \"ipopt\", {\"x\": x, \"f\": x, \"g\": x}, dict(opts, error_on_fail=True))
  >>> try:
  ...     S(lbx=0, ubx=1, lbg=2, ubg=3)
  ... except RuntimeError as e:
  ...     print(\"nlpsol process failed\" in str(e))
  True
  >>> try:
  ...     S(lbx=1, ubx=0)
  ... except RuntimeError as e:
  ...     print(str(e).splitlines()[-1])
  Ill-posed problem detected: LBX[0] <= UBX[0] was violated. Got LBX[0]=1 and UBX[0] = 0.
  >>> try:
  ...     nlpsol(\"S\", \"ipopt\", {\"x\": x, \"f\": x, \"h\": x}, opts)
  ... except RuntimeError as e:
  ...     print(\"No such field: h\" in str(e))
  True
  



